{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-40365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da6f1ce63be63dfa390b409ce3dff72df1b96143e4a6ba9ee649ce4976e7d179"} {"question" "q-40365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$U$ and $V$ only"] ["text" "$U$ and $W$ only"] ["text" "$U$ only"] ["text" "$V$ only"] ["text" "None of the sets is orthonormal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$U$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{6}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -\\frac{37}{6} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{37}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da6f1ce63be63dfa390b409ce3dff72df1b96143e4a6ba9ee649ce4976e7d179"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{35}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{37}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{35}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-40399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{37}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da6f1ce63be63dfa390b409ce3dff72df1b96143e4a6ba9ee649ce4976e7d179"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-81115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-4(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4})+2{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=3$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da6f1ce63be63dfa390b409ce3dff72df1b96143e4a6ba9ee649ce4976e7d179"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-83749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da6f1ce63be63dfa390b409ce3dff72df1b96143e4a6ba9ee649ce4976e7d179"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$U$ and $V$ only"] ["text" "$U,V,$ and $W$"] ["text" "$V$ only"] ["text" "$W$ and $V$ only"] ["text" "$W$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"} {"question" "q-140483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$U$ and $V$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069250/state.json" "file_sha256" "99498be01293104bafdcbb1f4af7cb4f8c81215fdcc06465a433dc86092e5d7e"}] "usage" {"q-40365" []} "basis" 2373 "retractions" [[17592186134208 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal